# A bare name in a COLUMN-NAME position (`sort`/`select`/`group`, and an aggregation's value
# column) resolves to a BINDING IN SCOPE when there is one, and that binding's String value
# names the column. `@name` still pins the column literally.
#
# THIS IS THE CHANGE THE PREVIOUS VERSION OF THIS FILE PREDICTED. It used to pin the opposite
# — a bare name taken literally, with a same-named column winning SILENTLY over the binding —
# and said the golden was expected to move when ADR 0028's decision was extended to these
# positions. It has been.
#
# Why it mattered: a library author's parameter names were reserved words in data they had
# never seen. `fn top(frame, key) = frame.select(key)` returned the frame's own `key` column
# and ignored the argument entirely, on any frame that happened to have one — same function,
# same argument, different answer, exit 0, and all three engines agreeing because all three
# were equally wrong.

w = "v"
D = dataframe({v: [2, 1, 0], w: [10, 20, 30]})

# THE CASE THAT USED TO BE SILENTLY WRONG. `w` is bound to "v", and the frame also has a
# column called `w`. The binding wins now, so this sorts by `v` and matches `sort(@v)` below.
print(D.sort(w).column("v"))
print(D.sort(@v).column("v"))
# `@w` still pins the COLUMN called `w`, which is what the sigil is for.
print(D.sort(@w).column("v"))

# Same in `select`: the answer is the `v` the binding named, not the `w` column.
print(D.select(w).columns())
# A String literal names a column too — a binding resolves to exactly this.
print(D.select("v").columns())

# THE LIBRARY CASE, which is the whole reason for the rule.
fn top(frame, key) = frame.select(key).columns()
print(top(D, "v"))

# A BINDING NAMING A COLUMN THAT DOES NOT EXIST NOW FAILS LOUDLY, where before it silently
# used the same-named column instead. This is the shape that used to hide the bug.
G = dataframe({g: ["a", "a", "b"], w: ["x", "y", "z"], n: [1, 2, 3]})
r = try(G.group(w).sum(@n).count())
print(r.ok)
print(G.group(@g).sum(@n).count())

# An aggregation's VALUE column resolves the same way — one rule, not a fourth one.
vcol = "n"
print(G.group(@g).sum(vcol).columns())

# THE CONTRAST that was already true: `with` is covered by ADR 0028, and always was.
k = 100
print(D.with({out: k}).column("out"))
